{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfumEqve6yEge4HCf4iDk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not reliably, and it depends on your Wrangler version. Historically, `wrangler deploy` did not create either one: you had to run `wrangler kv namespace create` and `wrangler queues create <name>` first, and deploy would fail if a binding pointed at a missing queue or an invalid KV ID. Recent Wrangler 4.x releases added automatic provisioning (in beta when I last knew of it). If you leave the `id` off a KV, D1, or R2 binding, deploy can create the resource and fill in the ID for you. As far as I know that doesn't cover Queues, so create those explicitly. Check the current Wrangler docs and your installed version (`wrangler --version`), since this feature has been changing."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2256,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2256},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8300183-492f-4119-8fef-e06020571c7f","uuid":"454179b5-e1ec-43d8-828b-59bb018c8bb3","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:46:32.413Z","request_id":"req_011CfumEqhVAkcmm6sQxxkd2"}
